# Document Picture in Picture (documentPictureInPicture.requestWindow) is a page of the site's own in
# a floating window: its player draws its own controls there, under Chrome's title bar. Arc's video
# controls don't belong on it. Chromium tells a page's observers about it the same way as a video
# going into Picture in Picture (WebContentsObserver::MediaPictureInPictureChanged), and the engine
# passed that on as a video: Cra laid its hover controls (address, ±15 s, play/pause, close) over the
# site's, two sets at once (GitHub #460). CEF only ever told Cra about videos.
# promise: #460
cra://open?url=file://${FIXTURES}/Pages/video.html
expect-log loaded file:
wait 2
cra://debug?cdp=Runtime.evaluate&params={"expression":"documentPictureInPicture.requestWindow({width:400,height:240}).then(w=>{w.document.body.append(document.getElementById('v'));return 'docpip:open'})","awaitPromise":true,"userGesture":true}
expect-log Cra cdp Runtime.evaluate .*"value":"docpip:open"
wait 3
cra://debug?pip=document
expect-log Cra pip document: windows=0
# Google Meet's Picture in Picture is this window. Dragged by its top bar half past the screen's left
# edge, it stayed there; a video's springs back into a corner, as Arc's Document PiP does (GitHub #702).
# promise: #702
cra://debug?docPip=before
expect-log Cra docPip before: windows=1 .*onScreen=1
cra://debug?docPipDrop=left
expect-log pip: document window let go at -
wait 2
cra://debug?docPip=after
expect-log Cra docPip after: windows=1 frame=10,.* onScreen=1
cra://debug?docPipDrop=bottom
expect-log pip: document window let go at 10,-
wait 2
cra://debug?docPip=bottom
expect-log Cra docPip bottom: windows=1 frame=10,10,.* onScreen=1
